@import "./compiler.sly"
@import "./build.sly"
@use Check

class SlytherInstanceManifest {
    What a check remembers about every instance, written as manifest.json in .slyther/instances and keyed
    by `kind:name`, contains for each one:

    - specHash: The hash of the #{Compiler::SlytherArtifact} that declares the instance and of every
      artifact it references that has no code of its own: a kind, whose hash is its rules, a plain
      artifact, or an instance of a composite kind. So a change to its prose or its args is noticed, and
      so is a change to what it leans on
    - rulesHash: The scope hash of its kind, which is its rules and what they reference, so a change to
      what every instance of the kind must do is noticed by each of them. Absent in a manifest written
      before version 2
    - spec: The declaration it was last brought in line with, as text: what specHash covers, written out,
      so an update may be shown what changed rather than left to find it by reading the code
    - rules: The rules of its kind it was last brought in line with, as written. Both are absent in a
      manifest written before version 3
    - parent: The instance whose expand emitted it, when it was not declared by hand
    - demandedBy: Every instance whose uses asks for this one, when its kind is demanded, so it is an
      orphan once none does, and never because one of them stopped
    - demands: What those instances ask of it, one `member (kind:id)` line per member, sorted: the spec
      of an artifact nobody declares, which its spec hash covers as a declaration covers any other
    - locatedWith: The args locate was run with, when it takes more than the id, so the instance can be
      found again once its declaration is gone or its args changed
    - contentHash: The hash of the segments locate prints, each as its path and its content, sorted, so
      where a segment is never matters, only what it holds. The content of a folder is its listing, one
      entry per line with a slash after the folders, so it changes with its structure and not with its files
    - signature: The `key shape` lines signature printed, when the kind prints them
    - dependencies: For every instance it references, the content hash and the signature it had when
      this one was evaluated
    - evaluatedWith: The hash of every script that reads an instance of the kind, as built: locate,
      signature, uses and expand, so what an instance looks like is known to have been read the same way
    - checks: Every step of the evaluate of the kind, keyed by its name, with the hash of its script or
      its prompt as built, and of who plays the judge of an llm step, since a verdict is only as good as
      whoever gave it, whether it passed, and the errors it reported. Absent in a manifest written
      before version 4, which recorded evaluate as a whole in evaluatedWith
    - writtenBy: Who last wrote it, as a cast of #{Compiler::SlytherRole} is recorded: where its code came
      from, never a reason to write it again
    - result: pass, fail, missing or orphan, and errors: what evaluate reported, every check together

    It records the version it was written with, 4, so one written before is brought forward: every
    instance is taken to have been brought in line with the rules of its kind and with its declaration as
    they are now, so what it already complies with is not updated again, only a change made from then on
    is noticed, and its next update carries no diff rather than one against nothing. What it was
    evaluated with is not taken as seen: a manifest of version 3 knows no check, so every check of every
    instance is run once, and only an instance that fails one is updated, since what it must do did not
    change with the version. Loading a manifest
    that does not exist gives an empty one at the current version, and it is saved with its version and
    with its instances sorted.
}

class SlytherInstanceChecker {
    Checks every instance a #{Compiler::ParsedSlytherScript} declares against the rules of its kind, given
    the root of the project, the folder the operations are built into, the path of the
    #{SlytherInstanceManifest}, the #{Build::SlytherArtifactManifest}, a #{Build::SlytherGenerator} and
    its options: compile, max, and fixes, how many times a failing instance may be updated with its
    errors, 2 unless given.

    An instance is every artifact whose kind has operations, identified by its name, and the instances it
    references with \#{} are its dependencies. Instances are checked in an order where every one comes
    after its dependencies, so a dependency is evaluated, and fixed, before what depends on it.

    An instance of a composite kind is expanded before any instance is ordered: its expand operation runs
    with its params filled as create fills them, what it prints is read with #{Compiler::SlytherExpansion}
    and written as `expanded/kind/name.sly` next to the manifest, so what it emits is committed and
    reviewed, and the emitted instances are checked as any other, in the order their references give.
    When what it prints is not valid, the instance fails with the reason and emits nothing. Since it has
    no code to evaluate, it is reported as expanded, with how many instances it emitted, and every
    emitted instance is recorded with it as its parent and reported under it.

    An asset is an instance of its own, keyed `asset:name`: one the script declares outside any trait,
    or inside a trait that some kind adopts, and never one of a trait nobody adopts. It is a file or a
    folder copied as it is, so Slyther brings it in line itself, before any other instance, with no
    script and no llm: it is missing when nothing is at where it lands, and created there with
    compile; it fails when what is there hashes unlike its source, whether the copy was edited or the
    source changed, and is copied again with compile, whole, over whatever is there; it passes when
    the two hash alike, and is kept when they did last time as well. A folder is hashed as
    #{Compiler::SlytherFolderSource} hashes it, and copied without node_modules or .git. To make a copy
    its own, a project drops the asset.

    An instance of a demanded kind is not declared at all: it exists because the uses of an instance
    that has code named it, as `kind:id`, and what those uses ask of it is the whole of its spec. So
    every instance with code is asked what it uses, the answers are gathered by the instance they name,
    and one that no instance declares is added to the graph with what is asked of it as its demands and
    the instances that ask as its demandedBy. It is then located, created, evaluated, updated and
    invalidated as any other, and the reference is recorded on whoever asked, so it is ordered before
    it, read as its context and compared against its signature. One declared by hand is kept and what is
    asked of it is added to what it declares, rather than replacing it, since what declares an artifact
    is never overridden by what uses it.

    What an instance asks for is only known once it has been written, so an instance that was just
    created or updated is asked again, and when it named something that does not exist, that is created
    first and the instance is put off until it does, rather than failing for a reference nothing has
    made yet. An instance created that way may ask for another in turn, which is satisfied the same way,
    up to three rounds deep, after which the instance is judged as it stands rather than the chain
    growing without end. When an instance put off comes back it is only asked again and judged: its
    create or update already ran and never runs again, since writing it again would only ask for
    something else, and its time is counted from when its work first started.

    An instance of a demanded kind that nothing asks for any more is an orphan, which is only certain
    once every instance has been brought in line, since an instance that stopped asking only says so
    once it has been updated. What is asked of every one is written as `demanded/kind/name.sly` next to
    the manifest, as the declaration nobody wrote, so a graph that grows out of the code is committed
    and reviewed rather than only inferred, and what is written for one nothing asks for any more is removed.

    An instance the manifest knows by a parent that no longer emits it, or whose parent is gone, is an
    orphan: it leaves the graph, so it is neither created, updated nor evaluated, its code is not
    touched, an instance that references it fails as it does when a reference is missing, and it is
    reported as orphan on every check until its code is gone, when it is forgotten. Nothing removes an
    orphan yet.

    Whatever the generator is asked about an instance, to create, update or evaluate it, is followed by
    what the instance references, one level deep, so its prose may lean on it: the rules of a kind, the
    prose of a plain artifact, and the args, the prose and the signature, when its kind prints one, of an
    instance. What the referenced ones reference is not included: an instance that needs it references
    it itself. The prose of a `ref` is only a pointer to its file, which the generator reads with its tools.

    It runs in one of two modes: with compile it is a compiler, and brings every instance in line with
    its declaration; without it, nothing touches the code, so it only evaluates and reports.

    For every instance, locate says where it is, run with the id and with whatever else its params ask of
    the instance, filled as create fills them, and so are signature and uses: when it exits 1 the instance
    is missing, and, with
    compile, when its kind has a create operation, it is created and then evaluated, otherwise nothing
    else is done. When it exits 0 but prints a segment that is not there, or a range of lines of a
    folder, the instance fails with what it printed and what locate must print instead, and nothing is
    created over it, since where it is was never established; the rest of the instances go on, so one
    kind whose locate is wrong never stops a build.

    Otherwise its content hash, its signature and what it uses are computed, and it is
    checked for change: it changed when it was never evaluated, when its spec hash changed, when the
    guidance of its kind changed, when its content hash changed, when what reads it changed, when any
    check of its kind changed, when it
    failed last time and compile is on, or when a dependency changed in a way it uses.

    The rules of a kind are its guidance hash, see #{Compiler::SlytherArtifactKind}: what a writer was
    told and nothing checks. A rule that changed is not a change of what the instance must do that
    the code must be brought in line with blindly, it is a check that changed: the instance is
    evaluated first, and only what fails is updated. And since every check is recorded on its own, only
    the checks that changed are run, and the verdict of every other stands as recorded, so tightening
    one rule costs one check per instance, never one rewrite. Whatever else changed runs every check.

    Whatever changed is brought in line with the update operation of its kind before it is evaluated,
    when compile is on and its kind has one, since the prose rules over the code and the evaluate of a
    kind only ever covers part of what the prose says: an evaluate that passes says the code met what
    that evaluate checks, never that it still says what the prose says. Three are not brought in line: an
    instance with no record yet, which has changed from nothing and is evaluated as it stands, one whose
    only change is what evaluates it, since what it must do is the same and the new evaluate decides
    whether it still complies, updating it only when it fails, and one that uses a key that is gone,
    which no update can fix.

    When the signature or uses of a kind exits with a code other than 0 for an instance that was
    located, a warning says so, since what depends on it is then compared by its whole code. Without compile, a spec hash or a rules hash
    that changed is not recorded as seen, so a later build still updates the instance, and neither is
    the text behind either, so the diff a later build shows is still the whole of what changed.

    For a dependency, the signature it had when
    this instance was evaluated is compared with the one it has now, key by key, into the keys whose
    shape changed or that are gone, and the keys that are new. What the instance uses of the dependency
    decides: a key, when that key changed; `*`, when any key changed; `**`, when any key changed or was
    added. Without uses, `*` is assumed, and without signatures, any change of the content hash of the
    dependency counts. A key the instance uses that is gone fails it without evaluating, since it is
    certainly broken. Whatever evaluates it and passes is recorded, so the next check keeps it, and an
    instance that failed is reported as failing until it changes.

    When the args of an instance move it, so locate was run with other args when it was last checked, it
    is located once more with those args, and whatever they still find is reported as an orphan and left
    alone, since nothing removes code. The instance itself goes on as any other, at where it is located
    now, and the move is reported once, by the check that notices it.

    Evaluating runs the evaluate operation of the kind: every deterministic step as its script with the
    id, or, for the check of a rule, with the segments locate printed, as it printed them, and the args
    of the instance in its environment, one `SLYTHER_PARAM` variable per param of evaluate, failing with
    what it prints when it exits with a code other than 0, then every llm step as its
    markdown followed by what the instance must do, its args and its prose as declared, and the segments
    of its code, asked from the generator for a verdict of pass and errors, each in a session of its own,
    so the verdict never leans on an earlier one. Every error the check of a rule reports is named
    after the rule, `Kind::rule: what is wrong`, so a fix and a report say which rule broke. Every step runs even after one fails, and the instance
    fails with the errors of all of them, so a fix sees every discrepancy at once and never satisfies
    one step by breaking the next.

    A create or an update brings the instance in line with what evaluates it, never the other way
    around: its markdown is told to leave the artifacts folder alone, and whatever it changed of the
    files the build wrote there is put back afterwards and reported, so no instance passes by rewriting
    its judge.

    With compile, an instance that fails is updated with the update operation of its kind, with the
    errors, and evaluated once more, up to fixes times. Every update of the same instance in the same
    check resumes the session of the create or update before it, so the fix continues the conversation
    that wrote the code rather than reading it again cold, as the fix of a script does in
    #{Build::SlytherArtifactBuilder}. A round whose update left the content hash as it was is the last:
    the code is not evaluated again, since the same code against the same rules earns the same verdict,
    and the verdict it already has stands. With fixes 0 a failing instance is never updated. An instance
    put off until what it asks for exists keeps the session that wrote it, so its fix resumes it as well.

    Create and update run with their params filled from the instance, by the name of each param: id is
    the name of the instance, errors is the errors it is run to fix, a param named as an arg of the
    instance is the value of that arg, and any other is the prose of the instance, unless it is optional,
    which the instance simply did not give; a param that is not optional and gets nothing must throw an
    error. Which params an operation is run with is the kind's to say, see
    #{Compiler::SlytherArtifactKind}: the instance only gives the args. A deterministic one runs its
    scripts in order, handed the params as their args, and throws when one fails.

    Any other is its markdown, executed by the generator, and is handed the params twice: substituted
    where the markdown wrote them in braces, and as the instance it is run on, named with its kind and
    followed by the value of every param and by what the instance must do, as declared. The substitution
    alone is not enough, since nothing makes a markdown mention a param, and one that never does would
    otherwise run without ever being told which instance it is working on. The errors are appended in a
    section of their own when there are any, so they are not repeated among the params. After either,
    the instance is located again.

    An update is told why it is being run as well: what changed, and, against the spec and the rules the
    manifest recorded, a line diff of its declaration and of the rules of its kind, each in its own
    section and only when it differs. The code was written against the minus side of those diffs, which
    is said outright, since an update that is handed the prose and the code alone reads the code as
    evidence of what is meant and leaves it as it found it: what the code still says of a line that was
    taken away is what is wrong, never the prose. An instance the manifest has no text for, which is
    every one it knew before version 3, is told the reason and no diff.

    What passes is reported as created, updated, fixed or pass, by what was done to it; what fails as
    fail; what is up to date as kept; and what is missing and cannot be created as missing. With max,
    checking refuses to create, update or evaluate more instances with the llm than allowed, before
    doing any.

    Every id list prints that the script does not declare is reported as a warning, except for a
    demanded kind: nothing declares its instances, so an id nobody wrote down is what it looks like
    when it works.
}
